              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for lawful, then the Latin for allowed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: rogātiō, rogātiōnis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rogātiō, rogātiōnis — \"a bill (put to the people); a request\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tribūnus rogātiōnem ad populum tulit. The tribune put a bill to the people."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rogātiō, rogātiōnis f., built on rogō, to ask: the magistrate asked the people whether they wished it. If they voted yes, the bill became a lēx."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rogātiō is the noun built on rogō \"to ask\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): the question put to the people. English rogation comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
