            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "abrogō, abrogāre — \"to repeal, to abolish\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Populus lēgem veterem abrogāvit. The people repealed the old law."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "abrogō, abrogāre, abrogāvī, abrogātum, ab + rogō: to ask the people to take a law away. English abrogate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "abrogō is built from ab \"away\" and rogō \"to ask\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): to ask the people to take a law away. English abrogate comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
