          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sanciō, sancīre — \"to make sacred and binding; to ratify\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Lēgem iūre iūrandō sānxērunt. They made the law binding by an oath."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sanciō, sancīre, sānxī, sānctum: to make a thing sacred and binding. Sānctus, holy, is its participle. English sanction and saint."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sanciō is inherited from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to make holy\" (EtymDB), and sānctus \"holy\" is its participle (Lewis & Short). English sanction and saint come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
